B4, B5, B6 is a Chow. If I discard B7, and draw B5, then I can form B3, B4, B5. That would be one meld. Then the remaining tiles would be W2, W3, T5, T8, T9, B8, B8, F2. Need two more melds and a pair. The pair could be B8. Then the remaining tiles: W2, W3, T5, T8, T9, F2. Need two more melds. W2 and W3 can form a Chow if I get W1 or W4. T8 and T9 can form a Chow if I get T7. So again, not certain. But this is a possible path.\n\nAlternatively, if I discard B4, then I have B3, B7, B8, B8. If I draw B5, then B3, B4, B5 is a Chow. But I need to have B4 in hand, which I just discarded. So that's not possible. Therefore, discarding B4 is not useful.\n\nSo the best option seems to be to discard B7. That way, if I can get B5, I can form B3, B4, B5. Then proceed from there.\n\nSo, the valid action to discard is B7. Let's check if B7 is in the valid actions. Yes, \"Play B7\" is an option.\n\nTherefore, the evaluation is to discard B7 to allow forming a Chow if B5 is drawn.\n", "output": "Evaluation: Discard B7 to allow forming a Chow of B3, B4, B5 if B5 is drawn, improving the chance to complete the hand.\nReason: By discarding B7, the hand retains B3, B4, and B8 (pair), enabling a potential Chow with B5 (4 remaining in the wall). This creates a clearer path to forming valid melds, while other discard options offer less immediate progression toward completing the hand.\nAnswer: Play B7"}
